  </div></div></div>
<div style="background:#fff;"><div class="cap-section"><div style="margin-bottom:8px;"><div style="margin-bottom:26px;"><h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0;">Frequently asked questions</h2></div>
    <div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-card);overflow:hidden;background:#fff;">
      <details class="sc-faq" open>
      <summary>What is a System in Package?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">A System in Package combines multiple die, passive components and interconnect within a single package, allowing a functional subsystem to be supplied as one component rather than as multiple parts on a PCB.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Which assembly methods can a SiP combine?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">Wire bond, flip chip and surface mount can be combined, with stacked or side-by-side die and underfill where required by the package design.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Can you shield an RF SiP?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">Yes. EMI shielding is available for RF SiP, together with transfer molding and package-level test.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Why build a SiP instead of placing parts on the board?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">A SiP can reduce board footprint, shorten interconnect paths, reduce the number of board-level placements and consolidate several functions into a single component.</div>
    </details>
    </div>
  </div></div></div>

